    const s3 = "From running machine to safety bicycle\n\nThe bicycle is one of the most efficient forms of transport ever invented, yet its development was surprisingly slow. The first machine that could reasonably be called a bicycle was the ‘running machine’ built by Karl Drais, a German inventor, in 1817. It had two wheels in line and a frame on which the rider sat, but no pedals; riders pushed themselves along with their feet. Drais’s invention was partly inspired by a shortage of horses following a series of failed harvests, which had made horse feed expensive.\n\nThe running machine enjoyed a brief period of popularity, particularly among wealthy young men, but it soon fell out of fashion. It was not until the 1860s that pedals were added, attached directly to the front wheel. These early pedal bicycles, known in Britain as ‘boneshakers’, had iron tyres and wooden frames, and riding them on the cobbled streets of the period was extremely uncomfortable.\n\nBecause the pedals were fixed to the front wheel, the only way to go faster was to make that wheel larger. This led to the development of the high-wheeler, or ‘penny-farthing’, in the 1870s, whose front wheel could be up to 1.5 metres in diameter. Penny-farthings were fast, but they were also dangerous: riders sat high above the ground and could easily be thrown forward over the handlebars if the wheel hit an obstacle. Riding them required considerable skill and courage, and they were largely the preserve of athletic young men.\n\nThe breakthrough came in the 1880s with the ‘safety bicycle’. Developed by several inventors, most notably the Englishman John Kemp Starley, it had two wheels of equal size and a chain that transferred power from the pedals to the rear wheel. Combined with the pneumatic tyre, patented by John Boyd Dunlop in 1888, the safety bicycle was far more comfortable and stable than its predecessors, and it could be ridden by almost anyone.\n\nThe result was a cycling boom. In the 1890s, bicycles became a common sight in towns and cities across Europe and North America. They gave ordinary people a new freedom of movement, allowing them to travel to work, visit friends and explore the countryside without relying on horses or trains. The bicycle also had a significant social impact on women. Cycling encouraged changes in women’s clothing, as long skirts were replaced by more practical garments, and campaigners for women’s rights saw the bicycle as a symbol of independence.\n\nIn the twentieth century, the rise of the motor car reduced the bicycle’s importance in many Western countries, although it remained the main form of transport for millions of people in Asia and elsewhere. In recent decades, however, the bicycle has enjoyed a revival, driven by concerns about health, pollution and traffic congestion. Many cities have built dedicated cycle lanes, and electric bicycles have made cycling accessible to people who might otherwise find it too demanding.\n\nMore than two centuries after Drais’s running machine, the bicycle remains remarkably close to the design established in the 1880s — a testament to the success of the safety bicycle.";
