    const s3 = "Signed, sealed, delivered: a short history of the post\n\nFor most of human history, sending a message over a long distance was a slow, expensive and uncertain business. Ancient empires developed courier systems to carry official messages: the Persian Empire, for example, maintained a network of stations along its roads where riders could change horses, allowing messages to travel remarkably quickly. These systems, however, were reserved for government use, and ordinary people had to rely on travellers and merchants to carry their letters.\n\nIn Europe, public postal services began to develop in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. They were often expensive, and the cost of sending a letter was usually paid not by the sender but by the recipient, calculated according to the distance travelled and the number of sheets of paper used. This system had obvious drawbacks: recipients sometimes refused to accept letters, and some people developed ingenious ways of avoiding payment, such as marking codes on the outside of an envelope that the recipient could read before handing it back unopened.\n\nThe transformation of the postal system in Britain is associated with Rowland Hill, a teacher and reformer. In a pamphlet published in 1837, Hill argued that the cost of transporting a letter was a tiny fraction of the cost of handling it, and that a single low price, paid in advance by the sender, would greatly increase the number of letters sent. His proposals were initially resisted by postal officials, but they gained strong public support, and in 1840 the Uniform Penny Post was introduced.\n\nThe reform included an important innovation: the adhesive postage stamp. The world’s first stamp, the Penny Black, bore a portrait of Queen Victoria and allowed a letter weighing up to half an ounce to be sent anywhere in Britain for one penny. The number of letters sent more than doubled in the first year, and other countries soon introduced similar systems.\n\nOver the following century, postal services expanded to include parcels, money orders and savings banks. Post offices became central institutions in towns and villages, and for many people the daily delivery was an important link with the outside world. The development of railways and, later, aircraft made it possible to deliver mail ever more quickly over long distances.\n\nThe arrival of email and digital communication in the late twentieth century led to a steep decline in the number of letters sent. In many countries, letter volumes have fallen by more than half since their peak. Postal services have responded by focusing on parcel delivery, which has grown rapidly with the rise of online shopping. Some have also introduced new services, such as checking on elderly residents during their delivery rounds.\n\nDespite the decline of the letter, many people still value receiving one. A handwritten letter or card carries a personal quality that an email rarely matches — perhaps one reason why greetings cards remain popular in the digital age.";
