    const s3 = "A world in a cup: the story of tea\n\nTea is the most widely consumed drink in the world after water, yet for most of its history it was known only in East Asia. According to Chinese legend, tea was discovered in 2737 BC by the emperor Shen Nong, when leaves from a nearby tree blew into a pot of water he was boiling. Whatever the truth of this story, there is clear evidence that tea was being drunk in China by the third century AD, initially as a medicine and later as an everyday beverage.\n\nDuring the Tang dynasty (618–907), tea became central to Chinese culture. The scholar Lu Yu wrote a famous book on the subject, describing in detail how tea should be grown, prepared and served. At this time, tea leaves were pressed into bricks, which were broken up and boiled. It was only later that the practice of steeping loose leaves in hot water became common.\n\nTea reached Japan in the ninth century, brought by Buddhist monks who had studied in China. Over time, the Japanese developed the tea ceremony, an elaborate ritual emphasising simplicity, harmony and respect, which remains an important part of Japanese culture today.\n\nEuropeans first encountered tea in the sixteenth century, through Portuguese and Dutch traders. In Britain, tea became fashionable in the 1660s, partly thanks to Catherine of Braganza, the Portuguese wife of King Charles II, who was a keen tea drinker. At first tea was extremely expensive, and heavy taxes encouraged widespread smuggling. When taxes were sharply reduced in 1784, tea became affordable for ordinary people, and it soon established itself as the national drink.\n\nBritain’s demand for tea had significant consequences. For many years, almost all tea came from China, which would accept only silver in payment. To reduce this drain on its silver reserves, the British East India Company began exporting opium to China, leading eventually to two wars in the nineteenth century. At the same time, the British began to establish tea plantations in India, particularly in Assam and Darjeeling, and later in Ceylon, now Sri Lanka. By the end of the nineteenth century, India had overtaken China as the main supplier of tea to Britain.\n\nThe twentieth century brought further changes. The tea bag, reportedly invented by accident in the United States around 1908 when a merchant sent samples in small silk bags, made tea quicker and more convenient to prepare. Iced tea became popular in the United States, while in many other countries new varieties, such as bubble tea from Taiwan, attracted younger drinkers.\n\nToday, tea is grown in more than sixty countries, with China, India and Kenya among the largest producers. Its long and sometimes troubled history is a reminder of how a simple drink can shape culture, trade and even politics.";
