    const s3 = "From hospital ward to world stage: the Paralympic story\n\nThe Paralympic Games, held every four years shortly after the Olympic Games, are today one of the largest sporting events in the world, involving more than 4,000 athletes from over 160 countries. Their origins, however, lie in a hospital in a small English town.\n\nIn 1944, the British government asked Ludwig Guttmann, a German-born neurologist who had fled Nazi persecution, to open a centre for soldiers with spinal injuries at Stoke Mandeville Hospital in Buckinghamshire. At the time, such injuries were often regarded as hopeless; many patients were expected to die within a few years. Guttmann rejected this view. He believed that patients could lead active and fulfilling lives, and he saw sport as a powerful tool for rehabilitation, building strength, confidence and social connections.\n\nOn 29 July 1948, the day the London Olympic Games opened, Guttmann organised a competition for wheelchair athletes at Stoke Mandeville. Sixteen patients, fourteen men and two women, took part in an archery contest. The event was repeated the following year with more participants, and in 1952 a team from the Netherlands joined, making it an international competition.\n\nGuttmann dreamed that his games would one day be held alongside the Olympics. In 1960, this vision was realised when 400 athletes from 23 countries competed in Rome, in what is now recognised as the first Paralympic Games. At first, only athletes who used wheelchairs could take part, but from 1976 the Games were expanded to include athletes with other impairments, such as visual impairments and amputations. The first Winter Paralympics were also held that year, in Sweden.\n\nSince 1988, the Paralympics have taken place in the same city as the Olympic Games, using the same venues, and since 2001 host cities have been required to stage both events. The word ‘Paralympic’ was originally a combination of ‘paraplegic’ and ‘Olympic’, but it is now understood to mean ‘parallel’ to the Olympics, reflecting the inclusion of athletes with a wide range of impairments.\n\nThe Games have done much to change public attitudes towards disability. Television coverage has grown enormously, and many Paralympians have become household names. Nevertheless, campaigners point out that disabled people still face significant barriers in everyday life, and that greater visibility at the Games has not always translated into improved access to sport at a local level.\n\nGuttmann, who was knighted in 1966 and died in 1980, is remembered as the founder of the Paralympic movement. His belief that sport could transform lives has been borne out by generations of athletes.";
